course: dictionary
lesson: 6708
title: The food pipe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The oesophagus is the muscular tube that carries food and drink from your mouth and throat down to your stomach. People also call it the food pipe or the gullet.
explain: «When you swallow, the food goes down a tube called the oesophagus (food pipe) into the stomach.» The brackets give you the everyday name next to the science word, so you can link the two.
explain: The oesophagus works by squeezing. Muscles push food down in waves, so «swallowing works even upside down».
fact: The oesophagus is a muscular tube in your body.
fact: It carries food and drink from the mouth and throat down to the stomach.
fact: Oesophagus is also called the food pipe or the gullet.
fact: Waves of muscle squeezing push food down, so you can swallow even upside down.
fact: The plural of oesophagus can be oesophagi or oesophaguses.
exercise: «The oesophagus carries food from the mouth to the stomach.» What does the oesophagus carry? | Air to the lungs | Blood to the heart | Sound to the ears | *Food and drink
exercise: Which everyday name means the same as oesophagus? | Windpipe | *Food pipe | Eardrum | Backbone
exercise: What pushes food down the oesophagus? | Bones moving the food | Your heartbeat | *Waves of muscle squeezing | Your lungs breathing in
test: The oesophagus leads from the mouth to which organ? | *Stomach | Small intestine | Brain | Large intestine
test: Why can you swallow when you are upside down? | Because the stomach pulls food up | Because you hold your breath | Because food is heavier than air | *Because muscles squeeze food down in waves
exam: Which sentence about the oesophagus is correct? | *Food is pushed down the oesophagus by waves of muscle | The oesophagus is only found in astronauts | The oesophagus carries air to the lungs | The oesophagus is in the head and holds the brain
